# This is a DELIBERATELY-VULNERABLE lab. The findings below are the lab, not
# accidents. Each is load-bearing for one step of the kill chain and has a
# hardened counterpart in aws-landing-zone/workload/eks.tf. They are suppressed
# so the shared Trivy gate (CRITICAL,HIGH) stays green on the lab as shipped; the
# blue-team overlay in defend/ removes the underlying misconfiguration rather
# than suppressing the finding. IDs verified against `trivy config infra`.

# AVD-AWS-0040 "EKS Clusters should have the public access disabled."
# AVD-AWS-0041 "EKS cluster should not have open CIDR range for public access."
# Intentional: eks.tf exposes a public endpoint so the attack box can drive the
# API. Scoped to a single /32 (0.0.0.0/0 is refused in variables.tf). A /32 still
# fires AVD-AWS-0041, so only a private endpoint clears it, which is the fix.
AVD-AWS-0040
AVD-AWS-0041

# AVD-AWS-0104 "Security group rule should not allow unrestricted egress."
# Upstream default inside terraform-aws-modules/eks (node_groups.tf egress_all),
# reached because a config scan downloads remote modules. Same accepted exception
# the sibling aws-developer-platform repo documents at length.
AVD-AWS-0104

# AVD-AWS-0130 "aws_instance should activate session tokens for IMDS."
# This IS weakness 2: the node group launch template sets http_tokens optional
# (IMDSv1 reachable) at hop limit 2, which is the SSRF-to-IMDS pivot. The
# blue-team overlay / paved road sets required + hop limit 1.
AVD-AWS-0130

# AVD-AWS-0031 "ECR images tags shouldn't be mutable."
# Intentional: the lab rebuilds and repushes the :lab tag on every iteration, so
# the repo must be MUTABLE. Not part of an attack path, just a throwaway-lab
# ergonomics choice; a production registry would be IMMUTABLE with digests.
AVD-AWS-0031
